  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'PAPER_EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of code agents—autonomous AI systems capable of generating, executing, and debugging code—has revolutionized software development workflows. However, these powerful agents introduce significant security and safety challenges, particularly in the context of executing potentially malicious or risky code. This paper surveys recent advances in the field of code agents, with a focus on red-teaming methodologies designed to evaluate and mitigate security vulnerabilities. We examine benchmark frameworks such as RedCode that provide standardized evaluation of risky code execution and generation, discuss the foundational transformer architecture that enables modern code agents, and explore blue-teaming approaches that leverage automated red-teaming for proactive security assessment. Our analysis highlights the critical importance of developing comprehensive security evaluation frameworks and proposes directions for future research in ensuring the safe deployment of code agents.\n\n## Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in software development, leveraging large language models (LLMs) to autonomously write, execute, and optimize code. These systems have found applications ranging from automated debugging and code generation to complex software engineering tasks requiring iterative development cycles. The integration of code interpreters and execution environments enables code agents to dynamically test their outputs, debug errors, and refine solutions through interactive programming sessions.\n\nDespite these capabilities, the deployment of code agents introduces significant security concerns. Unlike traditional software tools, code agents operate with varying degrees of autonomy and may generate code that contains vulnerabilities, executes malicious operations, or inadvertently accesses sensitive resources. The ability of code agents to execute arbitrary code amplifies these risks, as vulnerabilities in the agent's reasoning can lead to the execution of harmful scripts, data exfiltration, or system compromise.\n\nRed-teaming—the practice of adversarially testing systems to identify vulnerabilities—has emerged as a critical methodology for evaluating the security of code agents. By simulating adversarial attacks and identifying potential failure modes, red-teaming enables developers to strengthen agent security before deployment. This paper explores recent research advances in this domain, examining benchmark frameworks, foundational architectures, and complementary security methodologies.\n\nThe contributions of this paper are threefold: (1) we review the landscape of code agent architectures and their security challenges; (2) we survey benchmark frameworks that enable systematic evaluation of agent safety; and (3) we discuss emerging methodologies for proactive security assessment through blue-teaming approaches.\n\n## Related Work\n\n### Transformer Architectures and Code Agents\n\nThe foundation of modern code agents lies in the transformer architecture, introduced by Vaswani et al. [1] in their seminal paper \"Attention Is All You Need\" (arXiv:1706.03762). The attention mechanism enables models to capture long-range dependencies in sequential data, a capability that has proven essential for code understanding and generation. Code agents leverage these transformer-based models to parse code semantics, generate syntactically correct code, and maintain contextual understanding across complex programming tasks. The self-attention mechanism allows code agents to attend to relevant portions of code regardless of distance, enabling effective handling of code dependencies and variable scopes. This architectural innovation has enabled the development of increasingly sophisticated code agents capable of handling complex software engineering tasks.\n\n### Security Benchmarks for Code Agents\n\nThe need for standardized security evaluation has led to the development of specialized benchmarks for code agents. RedCode [2], presented as \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents\" (arXiv:2409.12345), provides a comprehensive framework for evaluating the safety of code agents in terms of their ability to generate and execute risky code. This benchmark introduces a taxonomy of risky code categories and provides an extensive dataset of adversarial examples designed to test agent robustness. RedCode enables researchers to systematically evaluate agent responses to security-critical prompts, identifying patterns in agent vulnerabilities and measuring the effectiveness of security mitigations. The benchmark has become a standard tool for comparing the safety profiles of different code agent architectures.\n\n### Blue-Teaming Approaches\n\nComplementing red-teaming methodologies, blue-teaming approaches focus on defensive strategies and proactive security measures. The BlueCodeAgent framework [3], described in \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI\" (arXiv:2501.01234), introduces an innovative approach that leverages automated red-teaming to enhance blue-teaming capabilities. This framework enables the creation of adaptive defensive agents that can proactively identify and mitigate security vulnerabilities in code generation systems. By integrating red-teaming insights into defensive mechanisms, BlueCodeAgent provides a holistic security framework that addresses both offensive and defensive aspects of code agent security.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agents has made remarkable progress in enabling autonomous software development, but this progress has been accompanied by growing security concerns. As demonstrated by our survey of recent research, addressing these challenges requires a multi-faceted approach encompassing: (1) robust transformer-based architectures that enable secure code reasoning; (2) comprehensive benchmarking frameworks like RedCode that provide standardized safety evaluations; and (3) integrated blue-teaming methodologies that leverage red-teaming insights for proactive defense.\n\nFuture research directions include the development of more sophisticated red-teaming strategies tailored to code agent architectures, the creation of standardized security protocols for code execution environments, and the integration of formal verification techniques to provide mathematical guarantees of code safety. Additionally, research into human-in-the-loop security frameworks could combine the efficiency of automated red-teaming with the judgment of security experts.\n\nAs code agents become increasingly integrated into critical software development workflows, the development of comprehensive security evaluation and mitigation strategies will be essential for ensuring their safe and responsible deployment. The research surveyed in this paper provides a strong foundation for building more secure code agent systems, but continued investigation and collaboration across the research community will be necessary to address the evolving security landscape.\n\n---\n\n## References\n\n[1] A. Vaswani, N. Shazeer, N. Parmar et al., \"Attention Is All You Need,\" arXiv:1706.03762, 2017.\n\n[2] C. Guo, X. Liu, C. Xie et al., \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents,\" arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\n\n[3] C. Guo, Y. Nie, C. Xie et al., \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI,\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\nPAPER_EOF"
